  "analyzed_vuln": {
    "function": "SSLHandshakeLayer::SSLHandshakeLayer",
    "file": "PcapPlusPlus/Packet++/src/SSLLayer.cpp",
    "description": "The handshake-message parsing loop has no explicit cursor bound and unconditionally computes `recordDataLen - curPosIndex` using unsigned `size_t`. With malformed/truncated record/message lengths, the cursor can reach or pass the available record data; the subtraction then wraps to a very large length, so `createHandshakeMessage()` accepts an out-of-bounds `curPos` as if at least a full handshake header remained. The resulting message is stored and `getMessageLength()` reads `length2` beyond the packet allocation (the observed crash in SSLHandshake.cpp:1302). The constructor also calls `getMessageLength()` twice rather than validating and advancing once. It must stop before subtraction unless `curPosIndex < recordDataLen`, require at least `sizeof(ssl_tls_handshake_layer)` bytes remaining, and validate the single computed advance against the remaining length.",
    "conditions": "A captured packet must parse through NFLOG/IPv4/TCP as an SSL/TLS handshake record, with attacker-controlled/truncated TLS record and handshake length fields arranged so the constructor's message cursor exhausts or exceeds the clamped record payload while its unbounded loop continues. The unsigned remaining-length calculation then wraps, propagating an invalid large `dataLen` from the packet bytes into `createHandshakeMessage`; a message whose `m_Data` is at/near the end of the pcap-allocated packet is created, and reading its two-byte handshake `length2` field triggers the heap-buffer-overflow."
  },
